course: dictionary
lesson: 12075
title: Climax: the peak moment
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A climax is the most exciting, tense or important moment of a story, film or event. It usually comes near the end, when everything that has been building up reaches its peak. «The storm grew louder, and then the boat hit the rocks. This was the climax of the story.»
explain: Look for build-up clues. Writers make you feel more and more worried or excited as the story goes on. «The clock ticked closer to midnight, and Mia's heart pounded faster.» Each sentence raises the tension, and the highest point of that tension is the climax.
explain: Climax can also mean the highest point of anything that has been building up. «The school play ended with a big song, the climax of the evening.» It can even be a verb: «The race climaxed in a dramatic sprint to the line.»
fact: A climax is the most exciting, tense or important moment of a story, film, event or piece of music.
fact: In a story, the climax usually comes near the end, after the build-up.
fact: «Reach a climax» means to get to the most exciting point.
fact: «Build up to a climax» means to become more and more tense or exciting until the big moment.
fact: As a verb, «climax» means to reach that most exciting point: «The concert climaxed with fireworks.»
exercise: «The storm grew louder, and then the boat hit the rocks. This was the climax of the story.» What is the climax here? | The calm start of the day | The morning after the storm | *The boat hitting the rocks
exercise: «The school play ended with a big song, the climax of the evening.» What does climax mean here? | The ticket price | The first thing that happens | *The most exciting moment
exercise: «The race climaxed in a dramatic sprint to the line.» Find and copy the verb that means reached the most exciting point. | dramatic | *climaxed | sprint | raced
test: «The clock ticked closer to midnight, and Mia's heart pounded faster.» Where is the climax of this part most likely to be? | After the story has finished | *Just before midnight, when the tension is highest | The very first sentence, before anything happens
test: Which phrase means to slowly become more tense until the big moment? | Lose the climax | Reach a climax | *Build up to a climax
exam: «The fireworks lit up the sky, and the crowd fell silent as the final bang began. This was the climax of the night.» Which word means the most exciting moment? | *climax | sky | night | crowd
